#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417380} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417396} {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417414} {:answer/id #uuid "563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417430} {:answer/id #uuid "96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417414} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26015440-1e7f-5a41-a515-cdb226ffde44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417401} {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417447} {:answer/id #uuid "88da9920-b611-5173-8083-4d93b60bb921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417466} {:answer/id #uuid "d873a68d-5dbd-5f44-ba5e-d5e3260cbab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417518} {:answer/id #uuid "e701ebf7-eb6f-578b-a734-909bfb567074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417447} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73c30be-aa49-512c-9991-68a5517212a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" :question/math-academy-id "q-213543" :question/problem "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfbb87-25ee-57d3-9593-65b9056166c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417413} {:answer/id #uuid "681eaafc-b181-55c0-a5c1-50fc24ec41d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417438} {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417491} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a402d1-6d3b-5a39-88ac-48a25a79c7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417509} {:answer/id #uuid "f66b2634-5d28-5976-9c01-6e76341a26f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65f5ba4-1fea-57cb-bbe2-73b81c041018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee6fe66d-09a5-518d-9a32-a533c9b3cc75" :question/math-academy-id "q-213540" :question/problem "![](images/70/70743ac83b876995835703b35fc133af2133960ea916f589d60c8ac5636ee4ef.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19eae037-9bb3-53cd-9405-bcdcf90085f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417390} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc5dc30-0af1-522a-98a7-9dc7cae28dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417393} {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417409} {:answer/id #uuid "82cc221d-48ae-5487-9285-67993b828548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417464} {:answer/id #uuid "c5013afa-3560-51a7-8ce8-80f8c3522c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27f213d-f5cd-55a6-989e-1054aab40f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213600" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,S\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊂Y,$ defined on $P(R)$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $4∣(x+y),$ defined on $N$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in R$ since $1=(-1)^{2},$ while\n\n- $(1,-1)\\notin R$ since $-1\\ne {1}^{2}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $({1},Z)\\in S$ since ${1}⊂Z,$ while\n\n- $(Z,{1})\\notin S$ since $Z⊄{1}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ then $(y,x)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣(x+y)\\,⟹\\,4∣(y+x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10238c-7d05-58b9-96e8-9c69a8801161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417685} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eb1104-92b0-5dcf-bd12-07d9a0132bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417691} {:answer/id #uuid "d5329d0a-dafa-5056-a8ca-478564ecacb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417693} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaaa408-6e32-550a-b040-2adb608fe8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417696} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e2579-8b62-5595-a1e0-8ecfe4516933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaaa408-6e32-550a-b040-2adb608fe8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417696} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b17b4e5-ae42-5c71-8493-ac975ab35b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c77aeb41-578d-533f-87d1-e33216f35c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84053" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0$ and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0972b4fe-a6de-5b28-91e1-b8f15d278acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417785} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417789} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd3bade-13d7-5747-b1e8-adac5e633beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417792} {:answer/id #uuid "505ee955-4b02-50a3-9047-a855589dfea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417796} {:answer/id #uuid "b7801bcd-fba9-5c75-b2ca-bfd94012877d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417789} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71b8e04-6ae9-5c68-998d-3aaf22c66038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e33f924-4eb3-55c2-bd43-bfeaf2283adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417790} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ca9a9-7bc2-5a65-be19-0f409201b0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417799} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b376f2-838b-5270-8108-30de4fdb1fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417804} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e22708-5653-5d53-b84a-eb8c3f50f2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417807} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4df2d7-22d1-55de-8deb-c49dbe88df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1b376f2-838b-5270-8108-30de4fdb1fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417804} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ed1e3e-e1e8-525a-96ba-0dbe6634885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b798def-9f4c-5c78-939d-4d3fa7b155b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418054} {:answer/id #uuid "36a1e53d-4736-58e7-9183-e39dca9e0ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418055} {:answer/id #uuid "4305aa10-6b9d-5a8d-b5e0-73d08076c6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418056} {:answer/id #uuid "64b20a39-4422-555e-853c-addaea9024df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418058} {:answer/id #uuid "6db79444-4f16-52ba-8432-cedcce75ea2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b798def-9f4c-5c78-939d-4d3fa7b155b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418054} :answer-field/id #uuid "8230f704-b679-555a-b3ea-6455f032cf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6641b4f-e396-5ff5-8410-bd9f53c4e8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98339" :question/problem "![](images/98/9836234d763ccb3da6b9ac0360dcb3662cacc5fc90d5ecc2dee20bf29ce0c77d.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is neither simple nor closed.\n\n- Curve II is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418167} {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418170} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418201} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418213} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418170} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-214430" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418172} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418181} {:answer/id #uuid "62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418184} {:answer/id #uuid "b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418203} {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214431" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{4}=∅$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418179} {:answer/id #uuid "81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418188} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418194} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418199} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418194} :answer-field/id #uuid "18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214065" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418195} {:answer/id #uuid "c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418207} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418211} {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418217} {:answer/id #uuid "e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418217} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214002" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15725fb5-a87c-5f38-b289-86f0d29427bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419436} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6398d0-50af-5610-9bee-df1bd0a9db26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419438} {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419469} {:answer/id #uuid "9f745b3a-8022-5a3b-be01-5e2c7e0f5ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419478} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbe5d78-91aa-5b37-8eb4-0ae8d7b2f02f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419469} :answer-field/id #uuid "11088ee4-ce12-5820-98c9-e84060e47cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2963275e-8991-5613-bcc4-d15bd0b82d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-85317" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of defective televisions manufactured by a company in a month\n- The grade point average of a college class\n- The average speed of a car during a trip" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of defective televisions can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The grade point average of a college class is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average speed of a car is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd3e57c-b365-5106-8e57-f1bf4d52d60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419459} {:answer/id #uuid "95e5938e-1f9f-5017-8e26-2fa55c8515fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419472} {:answer/id #uuid "af510a37-6a4b-5f1f-86bb-85f87f12de1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419482} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7eb87a-e66e-5486-8307-f6d72c9b0d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419485} {:answer/id #uuid "fc526f4b-7ce4-57af-8ba3-f8e218dab5b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd3e57c-b365-5106-8e57-f1bf4d52d60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419459} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89223e5-0c7d-5154-95e1-d5f1014440e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a75eec7-e20c-53a7-afe2-6cf4faf6becf" :question/math-academy-id "q-131311" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of network failures per week\n- The number of emergency calls received by a hospital every minute\n- The number of members of a band" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of network failures can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution.  The number of emergency calls received can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4df288-3de3-5913-80c1-82e310e1702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419433} {:answer/id #uuid "46289e15-5ed5-53d3-bfb5-9ed125b4b357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419451} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3664a7-7754-5c55-8474-a12a38720c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419457} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aceabe-b268-5af7-9a02-b0e18dbda4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419479} {:answer/id #uuid "ba905bc7-fb9a-5aee-98c6-60c46a28c15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3aceabe-b268-5af7-9a02-b0e18dbda4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419479} :answer-field/id #uuid "be7fedf2-2106-5faf-a3ef-836ba7bad8ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71278ae0-44f4-58f6-827b-4b9773ebb613" :question/math-academy-id "q-89222" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of emails received by a company's server in one week\n- The number of people who visit a coffee shop per hour\n- The height of $5$ buildings in New York City" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of emails received by a company's server can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one week).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of people who visit a coffee shop can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (one hour).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07160410-a73d-5491-ac7b-6d22b8d2282e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419431} {:answer/id #uuid "207e7398-1d2d-564f-924d-9241209c572e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419439} {:answer/id #uuid "93d90b19-71ca-57e8-931e-35cadfebadbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419471} {:answer/id #uuid "bd12d504-3244-5d03-bea3-5eee58c7022d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419487} {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc8a185-a6bc-582b-bc0a-ce5101e00aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68d38c1-253b-59e6-ad1e-93208422e4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131412" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The average points scored on a math test in a class\n- The number of orders a firm receives per month\n- The number of calls a firm will receive next week for help concerning a toy" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average points scored on the math test is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of orders can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. Again, the number of calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} {:answer/id #uuid "82d023e2-5bed-57ab-93f9-0516b548003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419465} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1e0977-7098-5e67-84ee-6897e0e3087c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419494} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaedfa0-ff2b-5bad-b874-0a1668ef36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419500} {:answer/id #uuid "f866e90f-16bb-5bd1-8827-73b69f68f662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda912e0-867b-5e29-86d2-2c6727a332af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411b05d-363a-5c38-b187-cabf795e2dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85316" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of visits a web page has per minute\n- The number of works of art created by a sculptor in a period of one year\n- The average height of the players on a basketball team" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of visits on a web page can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of works of art can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a year).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a85c7-16ad-5315-9321-6bb903fa5398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419605} {:answer/id #uuid "28bfae27-6981-5691-9a5a-62fdf2e3c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419606} {:answer/id #uuid "7596282f-3290-5e4c-b0bc-7c7fefc0d1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419613} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaf34e3-c318-5109-9b20-14ae45f46079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419621} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d05e4d-4d94-5fc7-aaf2-511184e63ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28bfae27-6981-5691-9a5a-62fdf2e3c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419606} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f52fc05-4e89-5fdf-9a0b-799264a128a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237ee44c-ddeb-50a9-b28f-87d4edfa4e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-212521" :question/problem "Given that $-21≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=12$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-21-(-12)=-9.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷3=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷9=-1,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $12∤(-9).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e51f6df-e206-53ac-8c6a-e8c968fdbab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419609} {:answer/id #uuid "56156d58-5991-5469-83af-0516720e5fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419611} {:answer/id #uuid "845c3642-d5b7-51cc-8c24-5c719388349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419616} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd62da4-495d-5a88-9b81-83fe4d43949a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419623} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e785cd-3ae0-5934-b08f-1e3d607da075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845c3642-d5b7-51cc-8c24-5c719388349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419616} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffa6280-410f-5863-a63a-238138cfb0ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dad68af-5609-5774-82ac-907f4e4304f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76962" :question/problem "Given that $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=7$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=19-5=14.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $7∣14$ because $14÷7=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=7.$\n\n- Note that $9∤14.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $2∣14$ because $14÷2=7,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cf90fb-e29e-510d-8cf4-93075f7dd6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420099} {:answer/id #uuid "7699eaf3-de8c-5053-898a-a4c8beee968f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420100} {:answer/id #uuid "99a9553b-c748-5118-a8d9-ca0c58ba4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420101} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ef7977-1930-5dd3-9b84-4d0b1b641d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420102} {:answer/id #uuid "eadf43e5-1274-5818-a95a-76c46e2e08da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a9553b-c748-5118-a8d9-ca0c58ba4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420101} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3ccce-1ebd-5f22-9b8f-6e3842468c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08bf8696-2d24-58a9-b5b4-856286859054" :question/math-academy-id "q-160646" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-1,3),(-2,2),(1,-4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,1)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(-4,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,1),(2,2),(-4,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b642c0e-8b84-5e4a-891d-2e1beb9ad37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420270} {:answer/id #uuid "593420ae-cc21-52d8-a8ed-d92dcd3ab3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420271} {:answer/id #uuid "b08a5b5d-77ac-5e98-91fc-de49ca37b422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420272} {:answer/id #uuid "fda4827e-23cb-5c38-a966-819a735816cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420273} {:answer/id #uuid "fdee9ac7-df88-5be7-8e6b-0c2c8b6fd156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda4827e-23cb-5c38-a966-819a735816cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420273} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed30b9dd-3daf-5001-88c4-b9152d2bad1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba992cb2-fd2f-5e4c-b95b-b82e7bb29b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-232536" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}+1?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in (1,\\infty ),$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb3e23cecdbd82803f5757827f21a424672bdde6da03782ca995d23e1c4d7b4.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(1,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(1,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420310} {:answer/id #uuid "486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420312} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420318} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f92ab3-81aa-5ff4-8365-5163fc68e5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420323} {:answer/id #uuid "faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420324} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-181669" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2},\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2}\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420308} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420309} {:answer/id #uuid "65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420313} {:answer/id #uuid "6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420315} {:answer/id #uuid "a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420315} :answer-field/id #uuid "981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181641" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{1-2x})=-2{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{1-2x})=4{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{1-2x})=-8{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{1-2x},-2{e}^{1-2x},4{e}^{1-2x},-8{e}^{1-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nB={3{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{e}^{-x})=4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nC={4{e}^{-x},-4{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=2,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420305} {:answer/id #uuid "1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420306} {:answer/id #uuid "43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420311} {:answer/id #uuid "c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420319} {:answer/id #uuid "dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420311} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e37987-951e-54e4-a566-e39dd06571da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181667" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420307} {:answer/id #uuid "671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420314} {:answer/id #uuid "872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420316} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420320} {:answer/id #uuid "d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420307} :answer-field/id #uuid "5beabf59-4d47-5db3-877d-e192dae60847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181706" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})=-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}},-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\sqrt{x},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420333} {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} {:answer/id #uuid "3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420336} {:answer/id #uuid "462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420337} {:answer/id #uuid "7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42463" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25029d74-6c31-52ad-8e40-faedc9e473db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420400} {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} {:answer/id #uuid "618e807a-28cb-599f-9e9f-ea6004064c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420402} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e5cb8-be50-53e9-bf24-a7374c9dcd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420403} {:answer/id #uuid "f2516513-8240-538a-acb7-6adbea086ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0ab4b8-5966-5883-ba7e-805d59df8d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5414df0-8556-5960-86b0-386e378b451e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213229" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\},$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420580} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420581} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420582} {:answer/id #uuid "dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420583} {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584} :answer-field/id #uuid "0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" :question/math-academy-id "q-181771" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045755e2-d591-54ee-8420-3178cdc956ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420588} {:answer/id #uuid "4cbca457-7b68-56e6-9d5d-1f73524010a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420611} {:answer/id #uuid "72cbce88-fa64-5e61-8cc8-f4a2aeb564ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420621} {:answer/id #uuid "844f10e3-5ed7-5640-92ef-91197a14bf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420631} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a983d6-5780-5e7e-be56-356425ae752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a983d6-5780-5e7e-be56-356425ae752f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420668} :answer-field/id #uuid "4355a00e-8da5-5b0a-966f-b3794b720dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00b6d002-6ca8-5b5f-a710-55bc4f02fb94" :question/math-academy-id "q-216443" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n\\ge -4$\n- $n=-4$\n- $|n|\\le 4$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\n$$\n\nNote that the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+7n+12 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n+3) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n\\ge -4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $n\\ge -4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=-4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=-4,$ then ${n}^{2}+7n+12=0.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|\\le 4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}+7n+12=0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then $|n|\\le 4,$ but ${n}^{2}+7n+12\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdd5fd1-d207-54b2-b6ad-11c0a56f64d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420592} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3107ee-9464-55c3-b2c0-aa35b357416e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420613} {:answer/id #uuid "60328983-7337-550c-99ac-442094612dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420614} {:answer/id #uuid "782a27b8-b034-5c8a-9112-6b1dee6f9a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420623} {:answer/id #uuid "ff258869-ea00-57cc-90ca-d8880f812d1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a3107ee-9464-55c3-b2c0-aa35b357416e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420613} :answer-field/id #uuid "53166bf7-c886-5b0e-983e-f872f31bc094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04618c03-3ca0-590d-b082-33d1176f03c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-172300" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an even number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is an odd number\n- $n$ is a positive number\n- $n$ is a multiple of $2$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number })\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is an odd number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=2$ is not an odd number but ${2}^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇏\\,(\\text{ is a positive number})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=-2$ is not a positive number, but $(-2)^{2}$ is an even number.\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an even number})\\,⇒\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is even, it must also be true that $n$ is even. Therefore, $n$ is a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e65e4f-7780-5d04-b434-5b982a0cc738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420590} {:answer/id #uuid "45df796e-175e-502d-b952-1965fb870644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420606} {:answer/id #uuid "8108a9c5-ed15-5e27-beb5-e7a64c2d63e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420628} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ad22a1-7d29-515b-8d15-5657d1156609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420659} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b29929-e1a6-5202-b99e-176a6e1f7c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e65e4f-7780-5d04-b434-5b982a0cc738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420590} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa13c5f-22a6-5392-8f0c-0c5ab3fb743c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10fd92b2-5e05-5850-b7d2-d8140b9fd04f" :question/math-academy-id "q-172315" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}=16$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $n=4$\n- $-4<n<4$\n- $|n|<16$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (n+4)(n-4) & =0 \\\\ n & =-4,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=4)\\,⇒\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=4,$ then ${n}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(-4<n<4)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2} & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\ne 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|n|<16)\\,⇏\\,({n}^{2}=16)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=-1,$ then $|n|<16,$ but ${n}^{2}=1\\ne 16.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0141819d-fce9-5dfe-8363-7f37fca55fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420587} {:answer/id #uuid "372fbdd1-3e8c-57d3-b2f8-c9c3f7462ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420602} {:answer/id #uuid "c263e106-7fef-58ab-9b12-f17370ec03a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420650} {:answer/id #uuid "c2efefc8-22d0-53d7-a45f-92c16998fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420651} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f792f1-74bf-5213-8686-058d2f686f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2efefc8-22d0-53d7-a45f-92c16998fbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420651} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f2ced5e-9852-5fb0-9bab-d51b27fc2767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28567992-ba7f-5623-ad07-13abe4be416c" :question/math-academy-id "q-172325" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x+1>4$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $|x|>3$\n- $x>3$\n- $x>4$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >4 \\\\ x & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(|x|>3)\\,⇏\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then we have $x+1=-3≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>3)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>3,$ then $x+1>4.$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(x>4)\\,⇒\\,(x+1>4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $x>4,$ then  $x+1>5>4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6db338-0cb7-51e2-ac01-95935810d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420596} {:answer/id #uuid "3ffe5c1b-c4bb-59cc-9a1d-6ddf172d759f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420604} {:answer/id #uuid "4a289173-5b61-589f-ba66-f52456ff017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420610} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad66aa9-02b1-539d-9ed6-634cc266d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa91d31c-1b7c-51c5-bbcc-d92d41570034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad66aa9-02b1-539d-9ed6-634cc266d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420634} :answer-field/id #uuid "380b7b8c-81dc-5fe3-b1fa-945984d0fd5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "390cc44c-87e5-5905-81de-8b4495a342fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-216459" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $f$ is a differentiable function\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$\n- $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇍\\,(f\\text{ is a differentiable function})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is a differentiable function but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne {sec}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1,$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1.$\n\n- Condition III is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16016d71-db47-5c29-a6f2-a697be2b8d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420594} {:answer/id #uuid "1c495edd-fe1e-5f10-9062-4276befeff1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420595} {:answer/id #uuid "80a3cd69-f340-5a70-9070-8ac5442c89a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420627} {:answer/id #uuid "a8debe43-b8b0-55ab-a68f-d6d8ea9b6da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420638} {:answer/id #uuid "acb5c849-cab1-5f29-a8bd-103025ea3634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8debe43-b8b0-55ab-a68f-d6d8ea9b6da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420638} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fdc134d-ac74-5fab-ae76-84aa43f0b703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b376045-26da-55cb-becc-db924f8d2dac" :question/math-academy-id "q-172302" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${n}^{2}$ is an odd number\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $n$ is odd\n- $n$ is a multiple of $3$\n- $n$ is a multiple of $5$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇒\\,(\\text{ }n\\text{ is odd})\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSince ${n}^{2}$ is odd, it must also be true that $n$ is odd.\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }3)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=7$ is not a multiple of $3,$ but ${n}^{2}=49$ is an odd number.\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n({n}^{2}\\text{ is an odd number})\\,⇏\\,(n\\text{ is a multiple of }5)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $n=9$ is not a multiple of $5,$ but ${n}^{2}=81$ is an odd number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e8bf70-2a60-5941-87e1-f5189ba873f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420589} {:answer/id #uuid "5970156a-402e-53cd-9465-784a6bc663ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420612} {:answer/id #uuid "78c0055c-c885-59cb-a2a0-5eac59047ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420624} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a7bdcd-3396-5c6e-a601-a161e66d71fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420652} {:answer/id #uuid "f78301a0-d9e6-54f9-81a3-411506bab620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f78301a0-d9e6-54f9-81a3-411506bab620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420666} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0157e5-7ad5-51d5-b39b-5c2649601c3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5904ff03-6479-54c0-a38b-31e73684000e" :question/math-academy-id "q-216449" :question/problem "$\\,$ $12∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $4∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $8∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $2$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $12.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $12$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $3$ and $4.$\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇒\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇐\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇔\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }4∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(12∣x)\\,⇏\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }8∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=12$ is divisible by $12$ but not divisible by $8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14e7d224-a6a4-59f8-8fdd-0ff468f330c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420593} {:answer/id #uuid "266cebd5-1df8-59d8-9d7f-5e97061db023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420597} {:answer/id #uuid "49f2aae6-e3f9-535f-82d1-b68b36c62b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420609} {:answer/id #uuid "ae91a6ae-e621-5467-9306-e9dd3d875c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420644} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd468a-9cc8-5dc8-8fd8-8420399559dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266cebd5-1df8-59d8-9d7f-5e97061db023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420597} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dddff4c-3d97-5829-a5f6-3f51ccd09496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ef2cacc-c405-55b2-8430-66b58129ad71" :question/math-academy-id "q-172298" :question/problem "$\\,$ $|x-2|=6$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x>4$\n- $|x|<10$\n- $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =-4,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x>4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x≯4.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of $|x-2|=6$ satisfies $|x|<10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(|x-2|=6)\\,⇏\\,(x=8)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-4,$ then $|x-2|=6,$ but $x\\ne 8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce83eb5-512d-54ec-a688-0337fd49e4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420603} {:answer/id #uuid "41c86782-467c-5503-8573-af98661637c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420605} {:answer/id #uuid "686fb17a-2611-5f1c-bdba-c5c19a1d121a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420619} {:answer/id #uuid "83a71146-83dd-5de6-95b8-b27059ff5408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420630} {:answer/id #uuid "bba394c5-1fc5-5d3a-b4f9-867f1cffef57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c86782-467c-5503-8573-af98661637c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8a18c8-c94d-53a1-9598-3f56ab83bdc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e1662ac-5299-5cc7-92ca-68567f121fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-183429" :question/problem "$\\,$ $20∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $10∣x$\n- $4∣x$ and $5∣x$\n- $100∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇍\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }10∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=10$ is divisible by $2$ and by $10$ but not divisible by $20.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $20$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $4$ and $5.$\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇒\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇐\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇔\\,(4∣x\\text{ and }5∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(20∣x)\\,⇏\\,(100∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=20$ is divisible by $20$ but not divisible by $100.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5084f6-69e4-520c-a5e4-b275a8c4693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420598} {:answer/id #uuid "48cc24d8-8ada-5963-8ea2-3d096ef3b9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420608} {:answer/id #uuid "67b27e1c-7265-5812-b062-c84b86be6606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420618} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc9b04f-28fc-5724-95bd-6fe1adf8f451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420633} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e878cb-1ec8-5097-aa5d-f6e3f76faa62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bc9b04f-28fc-5724-95bd-6fe1adf8f451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420633} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae23ffb-af9a-509a-99d1-3643141889bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9dd0c0c2-7652-5690-ae78-53d067f574fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-183485" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=2$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R$\n- $f(x)={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int 2x\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nMoreover, the differential equation ${f}^{″}(x)=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\int 2\\,dx \\\\  & =2x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D\\in R$ is another arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇍\\,({f}^{″}(x)=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ we have ${f}^{′}(x)=2x+1,$ and so the statement ${f}^{″}(x)=2$  is true but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 2x.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)={x}^{2})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne {x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f79458-4c2b-54d4-87ba-d97c53f760aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420635} {:answer/id #uuid "a41ae0db-cfac-58d5-a3cd-32a56a56b7c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420636} {:answer/id #uuid "b877dce5-c49c-5f58-9963-36f0cb4433cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420647} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ec35dc-2bc0-5873-a893-a0c100b00486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420657} {:answer/id #uuid "fec036cb-7688-5ab9-89fc-cd9daabe9e4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2f79458-4c2b-54d4-87ba-d97c53f760aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420635} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecfcee5e-24eb-56dc-ac84-c2b7a4109c97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4cdd56d-10e6-5abf-be98-585e1ce87428" :question/math-academy-id "q-172322" :question/problem "$\\,$ $a\\ge 10$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are sufficient for the above to be true?\n\n- $a=10$\n- $a>15$\n- $a>5$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a=10)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a=10,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition II is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>15)\\,⇒\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $a>15,$ then $a\\ge 10.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(a>5)\\,⇏\\,(a\\ge 10)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $a=7,$ then we have $a≱10.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723b0b18-8d2a-573d-8c7a-b060274c4be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420620} {:answer/id #uuid "79d4f89d-2406-5661-97b9-53a4d47d9095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420625} {:answer/id #uuid "c63ae798-9fea-58a3-a28e-246e0b2e732f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420653} {:answer/id #uuid "f2226bbc-d85c-51dc-b76d-73e6d3a402ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420660} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f07888-45ef-54ff-800d-2f443444b95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723b0b18-8d2a-573d-8c7a-b060274c4be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420620} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d6a9dc-fcc4-5ded-8f64-2cbe1ae88d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed1378b7-d665-5e7d-b9bb-42ac28cb1db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-216436" :question/problem "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\n- $x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0$\n- $x\\le 2$\n- $x\\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x({x}^{2}+x-6)\\le 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x({x}^{2}+x-6)=6≰0.$\n\n- Condition II is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇒\\,(x\\le 2)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEvery solution of ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ satisfies $x\\le 2.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0)\\,⇏\\,(x\\ge 0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-1,$ then ${x}^{2}+x-6=-6\\le 0,$ but $x≱0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca9ed22-18ac-5eeb-9f53-e13bcad40e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420591} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1c1196-eb9d-51a7-8da8-862fff9a154f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420599} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffe0ac0-8a5a-58ee-a4f9-48dcb58891b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420626} {:answer/id #uuid "81e54969-5398-5218-be00-7fbc3b23054c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420629} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d3a5f-e04d-5107-9d35-a0710f0ab915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d3a5f-e04d-5107-9d35-a0710f0ab915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420654} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdfba46-cdcd-52d6-a6f1-f3d15b8a74a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f653d451-6beb-59b1-affd-6d539e9553ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-216447" :question/problem "$\\,$ $18∣x$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary and sufficient for the above statement to be true?\n\n- $2∣x$ and $9∣x$\n- $3∣x$ and $6∣x$\n- $72∣x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is both necessary and sufficient: A number is divisible by $18$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $2$ and $9.$\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇒\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇐\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇔\\,(2∣x\\text{ and }9∣x)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇍\\,(3∣x\\text{ and }6∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=6$ is divisible by $3$ and by $6$ but not divisible by $18.$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n(18∣x)\\,⇏\\,(72∣x)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, the number $x=18$ is divisible by $18$ but not divisible by $72.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d52570-2557-595f-99e2-1b31c2d60dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420857} {:answer/id #uuid "4525744b-a33c-5d02-b83b-0cd4fd30e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420866} {:answer/id #uuid "8da70b03-f65d-5594-a91e-19d3b3531590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420880} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420889} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eda114-26bd-5178-9bcc-2a69974022c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78e6bb5-9afc-5b31-b499-d02078202d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420889} :answer-field/id #uuid "6033533a-9e89-5e03-a1f8-d82826d8190e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ae03bac-e2a8-5c3b-a5da-8c8f23590a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-127026" :question/problem "![](images/28/28a7eb32cfcd800c907e9f4af927eca7bd8616d36162682413db62a9feabefbb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fecf59674259d41222ffd981b1ab6d90c474fe2b0e37a6d5f7626f34536a6896.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/56/56a7fc09ed2a2a15b0949fa6041dc9c0ee6beb69f30b1a5c048afd8c57887d52.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420856} {:answer/id #uuid "3efe06e4-d0d0-5a68-8a81-622c991444de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420865} {:answer/id #uuid "5c350aee-ec57-55e6-825e-9ebcb6d46c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420870} {:answer/id #uuid "9073e798-9345-5c89-aa11-6f536f9781c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420882} {:answer/id #uuid "dd620493-1352-5d6a-ab86-993b4208f2b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05372253-4ce0-51d2-8c8a-6be80ef5e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420856} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e2f9e7-a7ef-5d8d-abe6-b747079ed849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cc7cc51-6281-53f8-b74f-55c2a0b2fa6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127024" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d8d85064253b9241b1fd93c22381897f23556d6e02c5c2c0161c94cee3bbdf.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is negative\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *in* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb4fd4068760bb9132052b26508f0e1e0c02c45d425770de804b17ce7c512919.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/46/46afd4cde01c95388c9daefbef0668ef696d5817ab9ea3614982edf58a9bdf55.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420862} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5a907e-06a3-50b5-82c0-14c80eee68f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420864} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb97c0d-6343-53ce-a7a9-c6e8df480d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420871} {:answer/id #uuid "b1686369-3dee-56da-b3d5-fd5ec9dbeb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420884} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e131d5-1b27-5110-aeb8-3ce97e516ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29facd25-cb5e-5912-bb18-0cf1f7a74908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420862} :answer-field/id #uuid "9864b2a4-0034-5f3d-ba1e-cf4c021c366a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e18756f-1e50-5f09-be71-26f001c4f68e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127027" :question/problem "![](images/af/afaec113101f1a63134d2b1cb5c9744b93f9b84dabafaa205780d2dc5b49a7bc.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/6b/6b24ece279cc480f20d7a4dbc65f7a5257af71f22688b1416c73e3b40673a321.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/9e/9e8527d26f0f2bd5372d8643f8f9321ec022bb0079e1fc5b23be4978f436613c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1675d456-4b28-58a6-bb2c-fed556137a4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420861} {:answer/id #uuid "30474622-ff1f-54c7-ae65-580701a7082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420863} {:answer/id #uuid "4902a853-9300-5dc4-9829-f0878517ea35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420868} {:answer/id #uuid "90a3ed78-ff8d-5b31-8fe9-66647638b119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420883} {:answer/id #uuid "c14caedd-1925-5e81-b3fb-2d1a39f46989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a3ed78-ff8d-5b31-8fe9-66647638b119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420883} :answer-field/id #uuid "406f40cf-3f8f-59a9-9607-1816ee0ec44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "765f030b-c4cb-527c-8c26-c947b0ace6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127028" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f1d25942ae680c068b587f38bfbc968c8850b8fa2db2003e994e1ec32dad892.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the clockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nII. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nIII. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7acc3028de66ae71ae4e425da639e51a357a77f2b0a345290592cac91ba17d3.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/04/04639a0074aa4607fa57bfe589aad1780f8da6f573d8589a68c2fdb569b5a129.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fca5176-1359-5ad8-b012-592ffa9b20b5" :knowledge-point/title "Cartesian Products With Three Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323a0eaa-c97b-52de-a3bc-ebc72ae2e5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420963} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1c11f8-7017-508a-a025-032d058eefc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420965} {:answer/id #uuid "9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420973} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ed4afd-f54e-59d9-bd0a-604a3f0d6358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420977} {:answer/id #uuid "df07081f-dcec-5eb4-b4c9-6e0e88404217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c53a0ac-9594-5d9f-871e-69dd75c6ab8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420973} :answer-field/id #uuid "f342874d-f251-593c-aa5a-839fe9ae8390" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c44203d-f0c3-504e-a004-3addf0955223" :question/math-academy-id "q-181956" :question/problem "If $A=\\{-2,2\\}$ and $B=\\{c,d\\},$ which of the following elements belong to $(A\\times B)\\times B?$\n\nI. $(-2,d,c)$\n\nII. $(-2,(c,d))$\n\nIII. $((2,d),c)$" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, $A\\times B$ consists of every combination of ${-2,2}$ with ${c,d},$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/98/98e7cfca515290b952ea5aefc9b2516a5cd6a68de0dc048c2075128e6c60b651.png)\n\nHence, the set $A\\times B$ is given by\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(-2,c),(-2,d),(2,c),(2,d)}.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot |B|=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian product\n\n$$\n(A\\times B)\\times B\n$$\n\nconsists of every combination of $A\\times B$ and $B,$ as shown in the schematic below.\n\n![](images/1e/1e96aeb1c5bfb1f9faa590a8eb8b01528a1ce9431a3f1ceac92b156a9bdde221.png)\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n|(A\\times B)\\times B|=|A\\times B|\\cdot |B|=4\\cdot 2=8.\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, we see that only $((2,d),c)\\in (A\\times B)\\times B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef0d8fa-6fbc-553b-b26f-6d47fc529632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421008} {:answer/id #uuid "a92c8a27-c90f-55ba-900b-012f540a5175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421071} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43712d-08c1-5314-bc53-7add8e989850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421075} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a82ac1-260b-5de1-9737-cad96665d7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421082} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5963c4-6280-5a68-adcd-4bcc40dceab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb5963c4-6280-5a68-adcd-4bcc40dceab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421100} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88db5da-74c7-5a6c-8f64-068574198241" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "03f10976-674d-5f7f-9c6a-ec5de8c4e5cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-183510" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=-1$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- $x=-1$ is sufficient for $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n- $x=-1$ is necessary for $(x-1)(x+1)=0$\n- For $(x-1)(x+1)=0,$ it is necessary that $x=-1$\n- For $x=-1,$ it is sufficient that $(x-1)(x+1)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{x=-1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{x=-1,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{(x-1)(x+1)=0}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0809c075-e1be-5d49-b1fe-5433114bcf16" :knowledge-point/title "\"If-Then\", \"If\", \"Only If\", and \"Implies\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093f27fc-7e6f-5b74-9e0f-56f1dcd8da8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421004} {:answer/id #uuid "27753478-b4dc-5f6b-9f5d-e4c8cf0b2741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421016} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf28c27-5628-51c2-afd9-674c7904768f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421037} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f026b-b1a3-571d-937c-f08cf77e8b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421042} {:answer/id #uuid "a241a06c-b9b1-53dc-aa4f-bc3f27232754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f026b-b1a3-571d-937c-f08cf77e8b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7423f83a-bd3f-57b5-a6e1-9ee4c5646676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0e0b252f-632c-5fb4-955e-f2c2686886d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-183448" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=2$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=4$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ implies $x=2$\n- If ${x}^{2}=4,$ then $x=2$\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ only if $x=2$\n- ${x}^{2}=4$ if $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the implication $P⇒Q:$\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{implies}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{if}\\,{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{only if}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=4}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{if}\\,{{x=2}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d40fcad-2b54-5f09-b54d-9a821f10c93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421006} {:answer/id #uuid "29ff0373-60cd-5181-92d4-2b66865dbf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421018} {:answer/id #uuid "988b9e54-2317-550f-8181-5d56c55e2bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421059} {:answer/id #uuid "e436142a-6fc6-52cb-bacc-09d95d051cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421095} {:answer/id #uuid "fe58e7c2-f1a1-57fe-86bd-e109566044a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d40fcad-2b54-5f09-b54d-9a821f10c93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421006} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cc20d1-1323-5c7b-9afb-5b2500b0cb08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50849cc0-0b42-5b52-abb5-e0bdf56880ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-183568" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $0\\le x<1$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $|x|<1$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $|x|<1,$ then $0\\le x<1$\n- $0\\le x<1$ whenever $|x|<1$\n- $|x|<1,$ provided that $0\\le x<1$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{|x|<1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{|x|<1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{|x|<1\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{0\\le x<1}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320b80df-22ed-5e98-ad73-2f1d0474fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421024} {:answer/id #uuid "41f5ede5-5a8e-58dc-84d4-9753affb83f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421034} {:answer/id #uuid "59e660dd-80d7-5d3d-9256-060d4013846a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421041} {:answer/id #uuid "9df674b4-222e-5226-8472-65f74b544b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421064} {:answer/id #uuid "addc3e96-7972-50b2-ae08-63e88d8f6e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "320b80df-22ed-5e98-ad73-2f1d0474fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421024} :answer-field/id #uuid "b060c187-cb85-5841-a5af-47c6d7c784ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78f9226d-96f1-55af-96d5-28d4a76505e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-183533" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $4\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is composite\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $4\\,|\\,x,$ then $x$ is composite\n- $4\\,|\\,x$ whenever $x$ is composite\n- $x$ is composite, provided that $4\\,|\\,x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)},\\,\\text{then}\\,{{x\\text{ is composite}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{x\\text{ is composite}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is composite,}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{4\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8ce566-6890-582f-a158-b42ec0895248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421005} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1c3f5e-ef37-5163-947d-6521fd9e875a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421045} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdb2a41-ea7b-512e-8918-cc67d1d238e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421061} {:answer/id #uuid "9d633d9a-da33-5e88-94db-67f637b7f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421063} {:answer/id #uuid "fde546f5-de83-5d7c-8c8e-c9d84f7e0b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d633d9a-da33-5e88-94db-67f637b7f7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421063} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5ffcf8-bc22-542f-9944-294204fb67c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bd7ef2d-fe07-5622-9cee-22e524115939" :question/math-academy-id "q-183566" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x=0$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ ${x}^{2}=x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $x=0,$ then ${x}^{2}=x$\n- ${x}^{2}=x$ whenever $x=0$\n- $x=0,$ provided that ${x}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P⇒Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n\\text{Whenever}\\,{{x=0\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{then}\\,{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{whenever}\\,{{x=0}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{x=0\\text{,}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{provided that}\\,{{{x}^{2}=x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40772f11-35fc-5f4f-bda3-7d228823b749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421258} {:answer/id #uuid "4526afa8-bdbb-5355-a82f-899c0edd5093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421261} {:answer/id #uuid "8096790f-b2a3-5ac1-9707-fe01377b65c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421272} {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} {:answer/id #uuid "c81d1e22-0260-5ad3-9abd-8fe77f612e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80faef7f-76e5-5c4b-8486-df12f7c6f160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421273} :answer-field/id #uuid "f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334252" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c8f651-7fad-58ab-9225-eb16c24dc65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421344} {:answer/id #uuid "90760cd8-7871-5634-b9b5-3531a983c1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421354} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3f0ee-7ced-5feb-9405-387aa70e3545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421361} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} {:answer/id #uuid "fec83f8f-fd76-54ba-94a1-1cd6f83935d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5dc06e-ed8a-53d3-bee4-a48767f1eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421378} :answer-field/id #uuid "763d9cb9-42d8-5b2a-baa3-fc94d026c898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c07a7ee-9fb6-5236-b864-38ec61e9b0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-214241" :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $P(N)?$\n\n- $A⊄B$\n- $|A|\\ne |B|$\n- $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- The relation \"$⊄$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since ${2,3}⊄{2}$ but ${2}⊂{2,3}.$\n\n- The relation \"$\\ne $\" for the cardinalities of sets is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive since $|{2}|=|{2}|.$\n\n- The relation \"$⊇$\" is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric since $N⊇{1}$ but ${1}⊉N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "589ce36f-fb09-55ca-ab3b-7f3aa6166186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421337} {:answer/id #uuid "661dd547-fab0-5221-9f01-e96464c7b3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421343} {:answer/id #uuid "a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421360} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8cf821-8c18-5dd6-be5b-f58b8b837325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421374} {:answer/id #uuid "d79a90aa-2e9b-5f04-b081-64d7cc3f75e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a186ab13-4673-549f-8754-6fcd73c7e651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421360} :answer-field/id #uuid "da731a85-b255-5017-87f3-60d7f37421d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a65003f-5b78-57dd-8ad6-282231fb787b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76164" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $R?$\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $xy>0$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x=2y$\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if $x-y$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $0\\cdot 0=0≯0.$\n\n- Relation II is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $2=2(1)$ but $1\\ne 2(2).$\n\n- Relation III is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $x\\in R,$ we have $x-x=0$ is a rational number.\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $x,y\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ is a rational number, then $y-x=-(x-y)=-r$ is also a rational number.\n\n- It is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in R,$ if $x-y=r$ and $y-z=q,$ where $r,q$ are rational numbers, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=(x-y)+(y-z)=r+q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040a02e2-1bb9-514c-903f-81f6a2d7ea14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421315} {:answer/id #uuid "17261494-8ee1-5a6c-a36e-65e458355a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 175921864